      "source": [
        "## Exercise 8: Vectorized XLA Distance Kernel\n",
        "\n",
        "Accelerators like GPUs and TPUs perform poorly with loop iterations. Write a purely vectorized, loop-free mathematical calculation to resolve standard pairwise Euclidean distances using JAX numpy primitives (`jnp`).\n",
        "\n",
        "Decorate your function with `@jax.jit` to instruct the hardware-aware XLA compiler to map your algorithmic execution paths down into bare-metal accelerator machine instructions."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 33,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "GQUCUlocaQct"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "@jax.jit\n",
        "def compute_cluster_pairwise_distances(X_train_jax, X_test_jax):\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "    Computes Euclidean distances between matrix variables of test samples and train samples.\n",
        "    Returns array matrix dimensions: (n_test_samples, n_train_samples)\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "    # Hint: Use matrix transformations to map (A - B)^2 = A^2 - 2AB + B^2 directly inside jnp primitives\n",
        "    test_squared_norms = jnp.sum(jnp.square(X_test_jax), axis=1, keepdims=True)\n",
        "    train_squared_norms = jnp.sum(jnp.square(X_train_jax), axis=1)[None, :]\n",
        "    cross_terms = X_test_jax @ X_train_jax.T\n",
        "\n",
        "    squared_distances = test_squared_norms - (2.0 * cross_terms) + train_squared_norms\n",
        "    return jnp.sqrt(jnp.maximum(squared_distances, 0.0))\n"
      ]
    },
